# Test: the looking glass answers a command the daemon does not serve as a
# refusal, never as an empty answer.
#
# VALIDATES: docs/architecture/api/birdwatcher-compat.md Section 3.1 on a real
#            daemon. With no `bgp` block and no `environment bmp`, the bgp-bmp
#            plugin does not load (ConfigRoots, bgp/plugins/bmp/register.go),
#            so the dispatcher refuses `show bmp peers`. protocols/bmp then
#            answers 502 with a body naming the command and the class
#            "engine error" (engineAnswer, lg/handler_api.go).
# PREVENTS: the failure answered as data. Until 2026-10-14 query
#           (lg/server.go) rendered a failed dispatch as {"error": ...},
#           which parsed, so protocols/bmp answered 200 with an empty
#           protocols object: "no BMP peers" where the truth was "this daemon
#           runs no BMP". The whole body is asserted, which also proves the dispatcher's own
#           error text stays in the daemon log and off this unauthenticated
#           API.

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
environment {
	looking-glass {
		enabled true
		# This test asserts looking-glass CONTENT over plain HTTP, so it takes
		# the documented opt-out from the TLS default.
		tls false
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp

# Wait on a page that answers from no command, so readiness says nothing about
# the refusal below.
http=wait:seq=1:url=http://127.0.0.1:$PORT2/lg/help:status=200:timeout=20s

http=get:seq=1:url=http://127.0.0.1:$PORT2/api/looking-glass/protocols/bmp:status=502:contains={"error":"show bmp peers: engine error"}
